## Why Networking Is Important

Networking helps devices communicate with each other. When we open a website, connect to a server, access a database, use SSH, or deploy an application in the cloud, networking is involved.

In DevOps, networking is required for:

- Connecting users to applications
- Connecting applications to databases
- Connecting servers inside a private network
- Accessing cloud instances remotely
- Managing ports, protocols, and traffic
- Understanding load balancers, DNS, firewalls, and CDN

## Protocols

A protocol is a set of rules used for communication between devices.

Examples:

| Protocol | Use |
|---|---|
| HTTP | Website communication |
| HTTPS | Secure website communication |
| SSH | Secure remote server login |
| FTP | File transfer |
| TCP | Reliable data transmission |
| UDP | Fast data transmission |

## Data Packets

Data is not usually sent as one large block. It is divided into smaller parts called packets.

Each packet contains:

- Source information
- Destination information
- Actual data
- Sequence/order details

Simple packet flow:

```text
Sender
  |
  | data is divided into packets
  v
[Packet 1] [Packet 2] [Packet 3]
  |
  | packets travel through network devices
  v
Receiver
  |
  | packets are arranged again
  v
Original data
```

## Packet Switching

Packet switching means data is broken into packets and each packet can travel through the network separately. At the destination, packets are arranged again to form the original data.

This helps networks send data efficiently instead of reserving one full dedicated path.

## Switch

A switch is a networking device used to connect multiple devices inside the same local network.

It sends data only to the correct destination device instead of sending it everywhere.

Example:

```text
PC1 ----\
        \
PC2 ---- Switch ---- Printer
        /
PC3 ----/
```

Use of switch:

- Connects devices in a LAN
- Sends data to the correct device
- Reduces unnecessary traffic
- Works mainly with MAC addresses

## Hub

A hub also connects multiple devices, but it sends data to all connected devices.

Difference:

| Device | How it sends data |
|---|---|
| Hub | Sends data to all devices |
| Switch | Sends data only to the correct device |

## NIC

NIC stands for Network Interface Card.

It is the hardware component that allows a computer or server to connect to a network.

Examples:

- Ethernet adapter
- Wi-Fi adapter
- Cloud instance virtual network interface

## Router

A router connects different networks together.

Example:

```text
Home/Office LAN ---- Router ---- Internet
```

Use of router:

- Connects one network to another network
- Helps send traffic outside the local network
- Chooses a path for data
- Commonly used to connect LAN to internet

## DNS

DNS stands for Domain Name System.

DNS converts human-readable domain names into IP addresses.

Example:

```text
www.google.com  ->  IP address
```

DNS flow:

```text
User enters website name
        |
        v
DNS resolves domain name
        |
        v
IP address is returned
        |
        v
Browser connects to server
```

## ISP

ISP stands for Internet Service Provider.

An ISP provides internet access to users and organizations.

Examples:

- Home broadband provider
- Mobile network provider
- Enterprise internet provider

## Edge Location and CDN

An edge location is a location closer to users where cached content can be stored and delivered faster.

CDN stands for Content Delivery Network.

A CDN improves performance by serving content from a nearby edge location instead of always going to the main server.

CDN flow:

```text
User
  |
  v
Nearest Edge Location
  |
  v
Cached content delivered faster
```

## Simple Networking Flow

```text
User Device
    |
    v
NIC
    |
    v
Switch
    |
    v
Router
    |
    v
ISP
    |
    v
Internet
    |
    v
Server or Cloud Application
```

